<BaseLayout title="Ludus Cyber Range" description="A self-hosted cyber range built with Ludus and Proxmox for repeatable security testing, detection validation, and incident response practice." currentPath="projects">
  <main class="page-shell">
    <article class="archive-panel markdown-page">
      <p class="record-label">Infrastructure</p>
      <div class="markdown-content">
        <h1>Ludus Cyber Range</h1>
        <p class="lede">I built this self-hosted cyber range to spend more time testing ideas, following evidence and practising incident response.</p>

        <h2>Why I Built the Range</h2>
        <p>I learn best by trying things for myself. In cybersecurity courses, I often spent a good part of a session setting up virtual machines, configuring endpoints, and sorting out networking and firewall rules. By the time I reached the exercise, I had already spent much of the time and energy I had set aside for it.</p>
        <p>Remote training environments brought a different frustration. Slow virtual machines and remote desktop sessions broke my concentration just as I was trying to follow a lead. I wanted a lab where I could pick up an investigation and keep following the evidence while it was fresh in my mind.</p>

        <h2>Why I Chose Dedicated Hardware</h2>
        <p>I wanted the lab to run independently of my everyday gaming PC. Dedicated hardware lets me leave the environment running without having to shut down virtual machines to reclaim resources for my desktop. Being able to reach the range remotely was also an important design consideration.</p>
        <p>Longer term, I would like to invite trusted cybersecurity colleagues to reproduce scenarios and investigate attacker behaviour together. Collaborative access is a future goal.</p>

        <h2>Why I Chose Ludus</h2>
        <p>Network orchestration was the main draw. I can define the virtual machines, network segments and communication rules that make up a range, then reuse that definition for the next exercise. The educational licence also gave me access to Ludus's enterprise graphical interface.</p>
        <p>I still need to understand which systems should communicate and why. Having those choices captured in the range definition means I can spend less time reapplying them and more time checking whether the environment behaves as I expect.</p>

        <h2>How I Chose the Tools</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Proxmox</strong> provides the virtualisation layer that runs the lab virtual machines.</li>
          <li><strong>Wazuh</strong> gives me an accessible, open-source way to collect and search endpoint telemetry. I was already familiar with it from cybersecurity training.</li>
          <li><strong>DFIR-IRIS</strong> gives me structured case records, evidence organisation and investigation timelines. I initially considered building my own incident management assistant for notes and evidence. That was turning into a software project of its own, so I looked at purpose-built case management tools and chose DFIR-IRIS.</li>
          <li><strong>Atomic Red Team</strong> gives me a way to run controlled tests mapped to MITRE ATT&amp;CK techniques.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Network Architecture</h2>
        <p>I put the attacker and victim systems on separate network segments. The monitoring and case-management systems sit on a third segment, where I can collect telemetry and document the investigation away from the two test endpoints.</p>
        <p>Segmentation was a security requirement for the design. If the test systems shared a flat network, they would have a direct path to each other without a deliberate routing decision. With separate segments, I can decide which traffic should cross those boundaries and keep unrelated paths closed. The distinction also makes it easier to reason about whether an event was produced by the exercise or by another system in the range.</p>
        <p>I designed the segmentation around recognised security practices, including limiting communication between systems to what they need. I also wanted the research environment isolated from my household network so that traffic from an exercise would stay away from personal devices and services. Household isolation was a design objective. The proof of concept verified the test traffic within the range, and I did not use it to verify the boundary with the household network.</p>
        <p>The proof of concept made that boundary tangible. The callback needed an explicit inter-VLAN rule between the test systems. That let me validate the specific network activity and trace it through monitoring without opening general access between segments.</p>

        <LudusLabMap />

        <figure>
          <img src={sitePath("/images/poc_test/ludus/ludus-range-architecture.png")} alt="Ludus range architecture showing segmented attacker, victim, monitoring, and case-management systems." loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Segmented Ludus range used for the investigation POC.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section aria-labelledby="investigation-poc-title">
          <h2 id="investigation-poc-title">Validating the Investigation Workflow</h2>
          <p>I validated the workflow with a controlled PowerShell TCP callback across the segmented range. Sysmon recorded the connection, I found the event in Wazuh, and I exported the evidence and closed the case in DFIR-IRIS. This confirmed that I could collect and investigate the telemetry. Whether an automated alert fired remained unverified.</p>
          <p><a class="text-link" href={sitePath("/incident-investigations/powershell-tcp-callback/")}>Read the PowerShell TCP callback investigation</a></p>
        </section>

        <h2>Testing Workflow</h2>
        <ol class="workflow" aria-label="Ludus testing workflow">
          <li>Deploy Range</li>
          <li>Create Snapshot</li>
          <li>Execute Controlled Technique</li>
          <li>Collect Telemetry</li>
          <li>Investigate Activity</li>
          <li>Document Findings</li>
          <li>Reset Environment</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Ongoing Research</h2>
        <p>Ludus will form the foundation of my ongoing cybersecurity research, including vulnerability reproduction, detection engineering, attack experimentation and incident investigations.</p>
        <p>I don't want the homelab itself to become an endless infrastructure project. Its value comes from what it enables me to learn, investigate and experiment with.</p>

        <h2>Acknowledgement</h2>
        <p>Thank you to the Ludus team for providing the educational licence I use in my homelab.</p>
      </div>
